[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Implicit vs. Explicit Animations and Animation Curves in SwiftUI"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"SwiftUI provides a powerful declarative animation system that lets you transition your user interface smoothly between different states. Understanding the distinction between implicit and explicit animations, combined with animation curves, is fundamental to building polished iOS applications."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Implicit Animations"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Implicit animations are the easiest way to animate views in SwiftUI. By attaching the .animation() modifier to a view, SwiftUI automatically animates any changes to the view's animatable properties."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"struct ImplicitExample: View {
    @State private var isScaled = false

    var body: some View {
        Circle()
            .fill(isScaled ? Color.blue : Color.red)
            .frame(width: 100, height: 100)
            .scaleEffect(isScaled ? 1.5 : 1.0)
            .animation(.easeInOut(duration: 0.5), value: isScaled)
            .onTapGesture {
                isScaled.toggle()
            }
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Explicit Animations"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Explicit animations give you precise control over when an animation starts and what state changes trigger it. Instead of reacting to state changes implicitly, you wrap state mutations inside a withAnimation block."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"struct ExplicitExample: View {
    @State private var rotation = 0.0

    var body: some View {
        Rectangle()
            .fill(Color.green)
            .frame(width: 100, height: 100)
            .rotationEffect(.degrees(rotation))
            .onTapGesture {
                withAnimation(.spring(response: 0.5, dampingFraction: 0.6)) {
                    rotation += 90
                }
            }
    }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Animation Curves (Timing Functions)"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Animation curves define the pace of the animation. SwiftUI offers several built-in curves to tailor the user experience:"},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":".linear: Moves at a constant speed from start to finish."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":".easeIn: Starts slow and accelerates toward the end."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":".easeOut: Starts fast and decelerates to a stop."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":".easeInOut: Starts slow, speeds up in the middle, and slows down at the end."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":".spring: Adds natural physics-based bouncing to the animation."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Use implicit animations for simple, state-bound view modifications. Use explicit animations with withAnimation when multiple independent views or complex state transitions need to animate together synchronously."}]
